# Component Builder Skill

## Purpose

This skill defines how the AI agent should create reusable UI components for the portfolio.

The goal is to create components that are:

- Reusable
- Accessible
- Responsive
- Consistent
- Easy to maintain
- Compatible with the project's vanilla HTML/CSS/JavaScript architecture

---

# 1. When to Create a Component

Create a component when:

- The UI pattern appears multiple times.
- The component contains meaningful behavior.
- Reuse improves consistency.
- The component has a clear responsibility.

Examples:

- Navigation
- Project Card
- Button
- Social Link
- Section Heading
- Project Tag
- Modal
- Contact Form

---

# 2. When NOT to Create a Component

Do not create a component for:

- One simple paragraph
- One unique heading
- A single `<div>`
- Trivial markup
- Every individual HTML element

Avoid over-engineering.

---

# 3. Component Requirements

Every component should have:

1. Clear purpose
2. Predictable structure
3. Responsive behavior
4. Accessible markup
5. Reusable styling where appropriate
6. Minimal JavaScript
7. Clear dependencies

---

# 4. Component Structure

A component should generally have:

```text
Structure
↓
Styles
↓
Behavior
↓
Accessibility

Do not mix unrelated functionality.

5. Example Project Card

HTML:

<article class="project-card">
  <a href="/projects/greenscore.html">
    <img
      src="assets/images/greenscore-cover.webp"
      alt="GreenScore sustainability app interface"
    />

    <div class="project-card__content">
      <p class="project-card__category">Product Design</p>
      <h3 class="project-card__title">GreenScore</h3>
      <p class="project-card__description">
        A gamified sustainability product designed to encourage
        eco-friendly habits.
      </p>
    </div>
  </a>
</article>
6. Component Data

Where possible, components should receive structured data rather than duplicating content.

Example:

function createProjectCard(project) {
  // Render project information from structured data.
}
7. Accessibility Requirements

Components must support:

Keyboard navigation
Visible focus
Semantic elements
Meaningful labels
Appropriate ARIA only when necessary
Screen reader compatibility

Do not use ARIA to compensate for incorrect semantic HTML.

8. Buttons vs Links

Use:

<a>

for navigation.

Use:

<button>

for actions.

Do not use:

<div onclick="">

for interactive controls.

9. Component Styling

Use design tokens.

Example:

.project-card {
  background: var(--color-neutral-variant);
  border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
}

Do not hard-code unrelated colors.

10. Responsive Behavior

Every reusable component must be tested at:

Mobile
Tablet
Desktop

Components must not introduce:

Horizontal overflow
Text clipping
Broken layouts
Unusable controls
11. Component Naming

Use descriptive names.

Examples:

ProjectCard
Navigation
SectionHeading
SocialLinks
ContactForm

Avoid:

Box
Thing
Component1
BlueCard
12. JavaScript Components

Use functions for lightweight component behavior.

Example:

function initializeMobileNavigation() {
  const button = document.querySelector(".menu-button");
  const menu = document.querySelector(".mobile-menu");

  if (!button || !menu) {
    return;
  }

  // Initialize behavior.
}
13. Testing Checklist

Before considering a component complete:

 Correct semantic HTML
 Keyboard accessible
 Focus state visible
 Mobile layout works
 Desktop layout works
 No console errors
 No unnecessary dependencies
 Uses design tokens
 No duplicated logic
 Content remains authentic
14. Final Rule

Build reusable components where reuse improves the product.

Do not build an elaborate component framework for a simple portfolio.

The component architecture should remain proportional to the project's complexity.